    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Baptist minister's son from Atlanta, King became the best-known spokesman of the civil rights movement after leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a>, which lasted 385 days and ended with a ruling against segregated buses.</li><li>Through the Southern Christian Leadership Conference he led nonviolent campaigns at Birmingham, in Selma and elsewhere, and the movement won legislative gains in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a> and the Fair Housing Act of 1968.</li><li>In his last years he opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and poverty, lost allies for doing so, was surveilled by the FBI, and was shot dead in Memphis on 4 April 1968 while supporting striking sanitation workers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>King was born Michael King Jr. In 1934, after a trip that took his father to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and to sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> leader Martin Luther, the father changed his own name, and his five-year-old son's, to Martin Luther King.</p></aside>
    </section>

